# Web colours

Use these colours on web pages and Home Assistant dashboards. Use a functional colour for its job first, such as the brand, link or error colour.

<%= colour_copy_status %>

## Web palette

Use these colours for supporting elements, such as an illustration or a custom card. Palette colours have no theme variable, so copy the value from the swatch. For text, links, borders, focus, errors and success, use the [functional colours](#functional-colours) instead.

Most colours have tints at 25%, 50%, 80% and 95%, and shades at 25% and 50%. Black has tints for greys. White has no variants.

The web palette has:

<%= colour_group_links('web', 'web functional colours' => '#functional-colours') %>

<%= colour_palette('web') %>

## Functional colours

A functional colour has one job. Use it only for that job, and use the palette for anything else.

In Home Assistant, use the civica theme variable rather than the value, so that the colour follows the theme colour and light or dark mode. In a custom card's CSS, write it as `var(--civica-border-quiet)`.

| Functional colour | Use it for | civica theme variable | In dark mode |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `brand` | The header | `civica-brand-primary`, which is the household's theme colour | The same colour |
| `brand` | Buttons, highlights and on states | `primary-color` | A lighter tint of the theme colour |
| `text` | Text | `primary-text-color` | White |
| `secondary-text` | Hints and less important text | `secondary-text-color` | `#CBD0D3` |
| `inverse-text` | Text on buttons filled with the theme colour | `text-primary-color` | Black, because buttons use a light tint in dark mode |
| `link` | Links | `link-color` | Follows the theme colour |
| `border` | Dividers and quiet card edges | `civica-border-quiet` | `#50565A` |
| `input-border` | Fields people type in | `civica-input-border` | White |
| `focus` and `focus-text` | The element with keyboard focus, and its text | `civica-focus-background` and `civica-focus-text` | The same colours |
| `error` | Error messages only | `error-color` | `#F0C6C6` |
| `success` | Success messages only | `success-color` | `#BCDACF` |

civica ships no variable for `template-background`, `body-background`, `link-hover`, `link-visited`, `link-active`, `hover` or the surface colours. Home Assistant draws the page with `primary-background-color` and cards with `card-background-color`, which civica sets to White in light mode, and `#17191B` and `#24282B` in dark mode.

